EN_0004": "{\\bf ", "TEXTBFOPEN_0005": "{\\bf ", "TEXTBFOPEN_0006": "{\\bf ", "TEXTBFOPEN_0007": "{\\bf ", "TEXTBFOPEN_0008": "{\\bf ", "TEXTBFOPEN_0009": "{\\bf ", "TEXTBFOPEN_0010": "{\\bf ", "TEXTITCLOSE_0001": "}", "TEXTITOPEN_0001": "{\\it "}, "render": {"prefix": "", "suffix": ""}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 2, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:8abd3fa9bc4baa7d4d46e13587d1fc7a4cf1b013f065b65ac75eaf03d0b4d1a5", "source_math_skeleton_hash": "sha256:b1d699bb79aa84978afe4e0f83fb6dab66943a42c9b2d99d76d2871f73c582ea", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:a1df0f76af7f4bda91e25fe297358831f776fc9b4e1d4127933c4d454975fd16", "source_text": "\n\n<STRUCT_0001>\nOk, so now we know that <MATH_0001> is an algebraic\nstack. What can we do with this? Well, it isn't so much the fact that it\nis an algebraic stack that helps us here, but more the point of view that\nproperties of <MATH_0002> should be encoded in the properties\nof morphisms <MATH_0003>, i.e., in families of elliptic\ncurves. We list some examples\n\n<STRUCT_0002><STRUCT_0003>\n<TEXTBFOPEN_0001>Local properties:<TEXTBFCLOSE_0001>\n<MATHSEG_0001> is smooth<MATHSEG_0002> is smooth<MATHSEG_0003>\n<TEXTBFOPEN_0002>Idea<TEXTBFCLOSE_0002>. Local properties of an algebraic stack are encoded in the local\nproperties of its smooth cover.\n\n<STRUCT_0004><STRUCT_0005>\n<TEXTBFOPEN_0003>Global properties:<TEXTBFCLOSE_0003>\n<MATHSEG_0004> is quasi-compact<MATHSEG_0005> is quasi-compact<MATHSEG_0006> is irreducible<MATHSEG_0007> is irreducible<MATHSEG_0008>\n<TEXTBFOPEN_0004>Idea<TEXTBFCLOSE_0004>. Some global properties of an algebraic stack can be read off\nfrom the corresponding property of a <TEXTITOPEN_0001>suitable<TEXTITCLOSE_0001><FOOTNOTEOPEN_0001>I suppose that\nit is possible an irreducible algebraic stack exists which doesn't have an\nirreducible smooth cover -- but if so it is going to be quite nasty!<FOOTNOTECLOSE_0001>\nsm